{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And Moses said to HaShem: And the Egyptians will hear, etc.</b> These verses require explanation, for 'And the Egyptians will hear' perforce must be explained as referring to the killing, as Rashi explains, and if so, he should have said immediately after 'And the Egyptians will hear,' 'And they will say to the inhabitants of this land' — so what is this that he said, 'And the nations will say'? If they are the Egyptians, why did he now call them simply 'nations,' and if they are the inhabitants of the land, why did he change their name? And besides this, the language is somewhat halting. And I saw fit to take support from what Maharil wrote on the verse 'But as I live, and the glory of HaShem shall fill all the earth': for a king of flesh and blood hastens to take vengeance for two reasons — either because he fears that today or tomorrow he may die and not see his vengeance in his lifetime, or because he fears lest his enemy flee to another place and he not be able to kill him. Therefore it says 'But as I live' — and from My side there will be no hindrance to retribution, and moreover 'the glory of HaShem shall fill all the earth,' and whither can one flee from My presence? Therefore He extended for them a period of forty years during which the sin would be purged, end of his words. And this is what Moses said: 'And You will kill the people as one man' — if You destroy them at once, then You would act like a single man who hastens to avenge himself on account of those two fears; but You are El and not a man — why would You hasten to avenge?" "And according to this, one may say that the Egyptians would not say 'Because HaShem was unable,' for they saw His power, blessed be He, with their own eyes. And this is what he said: 'And the Egyptians will hear' — when they hear of the killing, then 'they will say to the inhabitants of this land,' etc. And what he inserted between the clauses, the statement 'For You brought them out in Your strength,' etc., is the giving of a reason for the negation: for why would they not say 'was unable'? Because they saw 'that You brought them out in Your strength,' etc.; and therefore they would surely not say 'Because HaShw was unable,' but rather they would say something else. And to whom would they say it? 'To the inhabitants of this land' — to those nearby. And what would they say specifically to them? 'They have heard that You, HaShem,' etc. — for those nearby undoubtedly heard that You, HaShem, are El and not a man, and there is no escaping You, for 'eye to eye You appear, HaShem,' living and enduring forever, 'and Your cloud stands over them,' etc. And if so, whether encamping or journeying, the pillar of cloud is with them, and whither can they flee from Your presence? 'And You will kill the people as one man' — how would You do as the deed of an ordinary man who hastens to avenge in his wrath, and it would be beneath dignity and anger to ascribe such a measure to You, blessed be He. And this entire account is: 'They have heard that You, HaShem,' are El and not a man, 'and You will kill this people as one man.' And what he inserted between the clauses, 'who appear eye to eye, HaShem,' etc., is to explain the two attributes mentioned in the verse 'But as I live, and the glory of HaShem shall fill all the earth' — for they are also alluded to in the statement 'who appear eye to eye, HaShem, and Your cloud stands over them,' etc." "And all these verses speak of what the nearby ones will tell one another — that is, the Egyptians together with the inhabitants of this land. For they would not be able to say 'was unable,' because all of them saw and heard that He brought them out from Egypt with His strength. And this is what gives them opening: the more they see the power and ability of HaShem, the more they have grounds to lodge a complaint and say, how is it possible that He who is HaShem in the midst of the land, who appears eye to eye and whose cloud never departs, would kill the people like the deed of an ordinary man? But the distant ones, who heard Your report — from their perspective it could be that they did not hear of the entire mighty hand that HaShem did in Egypt, but only heard that there is a HaShem who swore to Israel to bring them into the land — they would not wonder at 'And You will kill the people as one man,' but would say a different denial. And this is what he said: 'And the nations will say' — that is, the distant ones — 'Because HaShem was unable,' etc. And even though You said 'And I will make you into a great and mighty nation,' and if so, in retrospect they would see the ability of HaShem — nevertheless, in the meantime the Name would be profaned, for many days would pass without a true Elohim before I become a great nation. Therefore 'And now' — immediately, in this generation — 'let the strength of HaShem be magnified,' for it is fitting and proper that also at this time the strength of HaShem be magnified to refute the words of the distant nations. 'HaShem, slow to anger,' etc. — to refute the words of the Egyptians who said 'And You will kill the people as one man.' 'And HaShem said: I have forgiven according to your word' — in several places they interpreted this verse as referring to the sin of the calf, and it means to say that during the entire time from Egypt until now 'I have forgiven according to your word,' but from here onward it is a reckoning, and I will not forgive entirely. And He gave a reason for the matter: 'For they have tested Me these ten times' — therefore I forgave them in the merit of Abraham, who was tested with ten trials, but from there onward this merit is exhausted; therefore I will collect My debt little by little. Even though in tractate Arakhin (15a) they also counted the sin of the spies among the ten trials, nevertheless according to the plain sense we may say in place of the spies, 'And they set out from the mountain of HaShem' (Numbers 10:33) — following HaShem. 'And on the level of derash one may explain:' 'For eye to eye' — in the merit that Israel received the Torah, which is interpreted in seventy faces, they merited 'the eye of HaShem is toward those who fear Him' (Psalms 33:18). And this is the meaning of the verse (Isaiah 52:8), 'For eye to eye they shall see when HaShem returns to Zion,' as our Sages of blessed memory said (Eiruvin 65a): 'Wine entered, secret went out — wine is seventy and secret is seventy.' For the redemption is 'the secret of HaShem to those who fear Him,' as it is written (Daniel 12:9), 'For the words are shut up and sealed.' And when the wine of the Torah, which has seventy faces, enters into Israel, then the secret of redemption will go out, and the glory of HaShem will be revealed (Isaiah 40:5). This is what it says: 'For eye' — the redemption, which is a secret ('סוֹד'), in the eye ('בְּעַיִן'), by virtue of the wine of the Torah — 'they shall see when HaShem returns to Zion.' And it is easy to understand." "And on the level of remez one may explain: 'For eye to eye' — in the merit that Israel received the Torah, which is expounded in seventy faces, they merited that 'the eye of HaShem is toward those who fear Him' (Psalms 33:18). And this is the interpretation of the verse (Isaiah 52:8): 'For eye to eye they shall see, when HaShem returns to Zion.' As our Sages of blessed memory said (Eiruvin 65a): 'Wine entered, secret went out — wine is seventy and secret is seventy.' For the redemption is the secret (סוֹד) of HaShem to those who fear Him, as it is written (Daniel 12:9): 'For the words are shut up and sealed.' And when the wine (יַיִן) of the Torah, which has seventy faces, enters into Israel, then the secret (סוֹד) of redemption will go out, and the glory of HaShem will be revealed (Isaiah 40:5). This is what it says: 'For eye' — the redemption, which is a סוֹד in the עַיִן, by virtue of the יַיִן of the Torah — 'they shall see, when HaShem returns to Zion.' And it is easy to understand."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:32.540715730Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}